-- title:
--   Assembling Φ_ℓ from a descended integral coefficient family
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field containing $\mathbb{Q}$ as a subalgebra, let $\ell$ be a prime, let $\zeta$ be a unit of $K$, and let $c : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Q}((q))$ be a family of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$. Three hypotheses are imposed. First, `PhiGenDescends ℓ ζ c`: for every $k$, the degree-$k$ coefficient of the monic polynomial $\prod_{i \in \mathrm{Fin}(\ell+1)} (X - C(\mathrm{conj}\ \ell\ \zeta\ i))$ over $K((q))$, whose roots are the conjugate series `conj ℓ ζ i` built from $\zeta$ and the coset data `cosetA ℓ i`, `cosetB ℓ i` applied to the image of the $j$-series `jq` in $K((q))$, equals the image under the coefficientwise map induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to K$ of `qExpand ℚ ℓ (c k)`, the series $c_k$ with all exponents multiplied by $\ell$. Second, each $c_k$ has all its Laurent coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}$. Third, each $c_k$ lies in the $\mathbb{Q}$-subalgebra of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by `jq`. The conclusion asserts the existence of a `ModularPolynomialData ℓ`, that is a polynomial $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ which is monic, has $Y$-degree equal to $\psi(\ell) = \sum_{d \mid \ell,\ d\ \text{squarefree}} \ell/d$ (equal to $\ell+1$ for $\ell$ prime), and satisfies $\Phi = 0$ after substituting $X \mapsto$ `jq` coefficientwise and $Y \mapsto$ `jqN ℓ`, together with the property that for every $k$ the $Y^k$-coefficient of $\Phi$, evaluated at $X =$ `jq`, is exactly $c_k$.
--
--   This is the assembly step producing the classical modular polynomial $\Phi_\ell(X,Y)$ for $X_0(\ell)$: the coefficients of the product over the $\ell+1$ conjugates, once known to descend to $q$-expansions with integral coefficients lying in $\mathbb{Q}[j]$, are recognised as integral polynomials in $j$, and the resulting packet is monic of degree $\psi(\ell)$ in $Y$ and vanishes at $(j(q),$ `jqN ℓ` $)$. It is used downstream for the splitting statement [`ModularCurve.PhiGen.splits_of_prime`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PhiGen.splits_of_prime), for the weighted support bound [`ModularCurve.ModularPolynomialData.weighted_support_le`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ModularPolynomialData.weighted_support_le), and in [`ModularCurve.exists_modularPolynomialData_evalSymm`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_modularPolynomialData_evalSymm).
